To slugify a title into a URL-safe string in C#, you can use the `System.Text.RegularExpressions` namespace to perform a regular expression replacement. This method will convert spaces, hyphens, and other non-alphanumeric characters into hyphens, which are commonly used in URLs.

Here's a C# method that accomplishes this:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

public class Slugifier
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Slugifies a title into a URL-safe string.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="title">The title to slugify.</param>
    /// <returns>A URL-safe slugified string.</returns>
    public static string Slugify(string title)
    {
        // Replace spaces, hyphens, and other non-alphanumeric characters with hyphens
        string slugified = Regex.Replace(title, @"[\s\-]+", "-");
        // Ensure the slug starts with a letter
        slugified = char.ToLower(slugified[0]) + slugified.Substring(1);
        return slugified;
    }
}

// Example usage:
class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string title = "Hello, World! - This is a test.";
        string slug = Slugifier.Slugify(title);
        Console.WriteLine(slug); // Output: hello-world-this-is-a-test
    }
}
```

